From f8c62414ec307fbd962b3e52ebef6668dc992f24 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 16:45:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Drop the Safety Hub password check that can never run

Safety check opened on a row reading "Can't check passwords in your
Google Account", explaining that to check for compromised passwords you
should sign in to your Google Account, under a button marked "Sign in to
Chrome". None of that is possible here. The row could only ever report
that it had failed, and the button led to a flow this build removed.

It also dragged the whole page down with it. Safety Hub summarises its
modules, and with one permanently unable to check, the summary sat at a
warning. With the row gone the page opens on "Your browser looks good",
which is now an honest answer rather than a hidden failure.

The unified module goes too, being the same account check with the local
list folded in, and the account data source went with them: it was the
only thing in Safety Hub reaching for the SigninManager.

What remains is what still works: the update check, unused permissions,
notification review, and the local password module when its own feature
flag is on. Passwords saved on the device are unaffected, and the autofill
and password settings elsewhere in Settings are untouched. This removes a
report that could not be produced, not a protection.
